# TypeScript

TypeScript is not decoration. It's the contract that makes refactoring safe. These rules assume `strict: true` and treat the type system as a load-bearing structure.

## Compiler settings

`tsconfig.app.json` must enable:

```jsonc
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "strict": true,
    "noUncheckedIndexedAccess": true,    // arr[0] is T | undefined
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true
  }
}
```

`npm run build` runs `tsc -b` and **must** pass with zero errors. There is no `// @ts-ignore` budget.

## `any` is forbidden

`any` deletes type safety silently. Use `unknown` and narrow.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG
function handle(data: any) { return data.user.name }

// ✅ CORRECT
function handle(data: unknown): string {
  const parsed = ResponseSchema.parse(data)
  return parsed.user.name
}
```

Allowed `any` exceptions (all require an inline comment justifying the use):

- Third-party type holes where a wrapping function casts on the boundary
- Test factories where the value is then asserted

`@ts-expect-error` is preferred over `@ts-ignore` (it errors when the underlying problem is fixed). Always include a one-line reason.

## `type` vs `interface`

**Default to `type`.** Use **`interface`** only for declaration merging (rare — extending a third-party `Window`, augmenting an ambient module).

```ts
// ✅ default
type User = { id: string; name: string }
type AsyncStatus = "loading" | "error" | "empty" | "success"
type Result<T, E = Error> = { ok: true; value: T } | { ok: false; error: E }
```

Rationale: `type` supports unions/intersections/mapped types/conditional types; `interface` is structurally identical for object shapes but inconsistent across union/intersection edges. The one-rule-for-everything keeps reviews shorter and the codebase consistent.

## Type-only imports

Always use `import type` for type-only imports. With `verbatimModuleSyntax: true`, the build relies on it.

```ts
import type { User } from "@/modules/tenant"
import { useTenantUsersScreen } from "@/modules/tenant"
```

## Narrowing over casting

`as` is a code smell. Reach for type guards.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG
const user = data as User

// ✅ CORRECT — runtime + compile-time guard
function isUser(x: unknown): x is User {
  return typeof x === "object" && x !== null
    && "id" in x && typeof (x as User).id === "string"
}

if (isUser(data)) {
  // data: User here
}
```

For external data, prefer **zod** schemas over hand-written guards (see `validation.md`).

### Assertion functions for invariants

For invariants that must hold or the whole flow is broken, an assertion function gives narrower types throughout the rest of the scope:

```ts
function assertNonNull<T>(x: T | null | undefined, name: string): asserts x is T {
  if (x == null) throw new Error(`${name} is required`)
}

function placeOrder(userId: string | null) {
  assertNonNull(userId, "userId")
  // userId: string from here on
  return httpClient.post("/orders", { userId })
}
```

Use assertion functions sparingly — they throw. For recoverable nulls, narrow with a guard or zod.

### The only acceptable `as`

- `as const` (for literal-narrowing) — encouraged
- `as unknown as T` when bridging two unrelated types in test setup — discouraged
- DOM event targets where the API forces a cast — annotate why

## `as const` for literals

`as const` freezes the type to the narrowest possible literal. Use it for query keys, route paths, allowlists, and discriminator strings.

```ts
// Query keys (see service-patterns.md)
export const productKeys = {
  all: ["products"] as const,
  list: (filters: Filters) => [...productKeys.all, "list", filters] as const,
}
// productKeys.all has type readonly ["products"], not string[]

// Allowlists
const PLANS = ["free", "pro", "enterprise"] as const
type Plan = typeof PLANS[number]            // "free" | "pro" | "enterprise"

// Route paths
const ROUTES = {
  tenantHome: "/tenant",
  pos: "/tenant/pos/sell",
} as const
type AppPath = typeof ROUTES[keyof typeof ROUTES]
```

Without `as const`, the inferred type widens to `string`, which loses the literal union and breaks exhaustiveness checks.

## Discriminated unions for state

Async state, form state, and result types must use discriminated unions, not optional fields. The compiler then forces every branch to be handled.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG
type ScreenState = { loading: boolean; error?: Error; data?: User[] }

// ✅ CORRECT
type ScreenState =
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "error"; error: Error }
  | { status: "empty" }
  | { status: "success"; users: User[] }

function render(state: ScreenState) {
  switch (state.status) {
    case "loading": return <Spinner />
    case "error":   return <Err msg={state.error.message} />  // narrowed
    case "empty":   return <Empty />
    case "success": return <List users={state.users} />       // narrowed
  }
}
```

Pick **one** discriminator per union (`status`, `kind`, or `type`) and stay consistent across the codebase.

## `Readonly` and `readonly`

Mark API response types and props as `Readonly` when the consumer must not mutate:

```ts
type Props = Readonly<{ user: User; onSave: (u: User) => void }>
type ApiUser = Readonly<{ id: string; name: string }>
```

Use `readonly T[]` for arrays you don't intend to mutate. Pick one style (`readonly T[]` over `ReadonlyArray<T>`) and stay consistent.

## Generic constraints

Always constrain generics — unconstrained generics are usually a sign of an incorrect abstraction.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG
function pick<T, K>(obj: T, key: K): T[K]  // K not constrained to keyof T

// ✅ CORRECT
function pick<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K]
```

Useful generic constraints:

- `<T extends object>` — only objects
- `<T extends { id: string }>` — has an id
- `<T extends Record<string, unknown>>` — string-keyed
- `<T extends (...args: never[]) => unknown>` — any callable

## Branded types for IDs

A `string` for a user id and a `string` for an order id should not be interchangeable.

```ts
type Brand<T, B> = T & { readonly __brand: B }
export type UserId = Brand<string, "UserId">
export type OrderId = Brand<string, "OrderId">

export const UserId = (s: string): UserId => s as UserId
```

Use branded types for any id that crosses a layer boundary.

## Utility types

Reach for built-ins before writing your own: `Partial`, `Required`, `Readonly`, `Pick`, `Omit`, `Record`, `NonNullable`, `Awaited`, `ReturnType`, `Parameters`. Compose them; don't reimplement them.

```ts
type UpdateUserPayload = Partial<Pick<User, "name" | "email">>
type UserSummary = Pick<User, "id" | "name">
type CreateUser = Omit<User, "id" | "createdAt">
```

Useful custom utilities (write once in `shared/types/utility.ts`):

```ts
// Make specific keys optional
export type PartialBy<T, K extends keyof T> = Omit<T, K> & Partial<Pick<T, K>>

// Make specific keys required
export type RequiredBy<T, K extends keyof T> = Omit<T, K> & Required<Pick<T, K>>

// Drop the readonly modifier (rarely useful, but exists)
export type Mutable<T> = { -readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] }

// Strip nullability from all fields
export type NonNullableFields<T> = { [K in keyof T]: NonNullable<T[K]> }
```

## Enums

**Don't use `enum`.** Use string-literal unions or `as const` objects.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG
enum Role { Admin = "admin", User = "user" }

// ✅ CORRECT
type Role = "admin" | "cashier" | "owner"
const ROLES = ["admin", "cashier", "owner"] as const
type Role2 = typeof ROLES[number]
```

Rationale: `enum` emits runtime code, breaks tree-shaking, and behaves inconsistently between numeric and string forms.

## Function signatures

Prefer explicit return types on exported functions. Inferred return types on local helpers are fine.

```ts
// ✅ exported — explicit
export function toUserMessage(error: unknown): string { /* ... */ }
export async function fetchProduct(id: ProductId): Promise<Product> { /* ... */ }

// ✅ local — inferred
const items = users.map(u => u.name)
```

For hooks, the inferred return type is usually richer than what you'd write by hand — let TS infer unless the shape needs to be a named export (e.g. a reusable `UseToggleReturn`).

## Don't reach for `Function` or `Object`

`Function` and `Object` are too loose to mean anything. Use `(...args: never[]) => unknown` for callables and `Record<string, unknown>` for plain objects.

## Don't use `!` (non-null assertion)

`!` lies to the compiler. The runtime can still be `null`.

```ts
// ❌ WRONG
const name = user!.name

// ✅ CORRECT — narrow first
if (!user) throw new Error("user required")
const name = user.name

// ✅ CORRECT — assertion function (see "Narrowing")
assertNonNull(user, "user")
const name = user.name
```

The only place `!` is tolerable is `document.getElementById("root")!` at app boot — and even there, an explicit error message is better.

## Type-safe environment

`import.meta.env.VITE_*` should be typed once in `vite-env.d.ts` and parsed through zod in `core/config/env.ts`:

```ts
// vite-env.d.ts
interface ImportMetaEnv {
  readonly VITE_API_BASE_URL: string
  readonly VITE_FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT: string
}

// core/config/env.ts
export const env = EnvSchema.parse(import.meta.env)
```

Don't sprinkle `import.meta.env` reads across the codebase. See `config.md`.

